<dialog id="guide-dialog" class="guide-dialog"><div class="dialog-header"><div><p class="eyebrow">Kinetic Core field guide</p><h2>You're the flight crew.</h2></div><button class="icon-button" id="close-guide" aria-label="Close flight guide">×</button></div><div class="guide-content"><section><h3>01 / Build a vehicle</h3><p>Select a command capsule, service module and two launch stages. Mass, ideal delta-v and liftoff thrust-to-weight respond to your choices. These are fictional training designs with real physical units.</p></section><section><h3>02 / Reach orbit</h3><p>Guidance steers the rocket using actual thrust; it never changes your position or velocity directly. Keep it on for your first flight. You control staging and the throttle ceiling.</p><dl class="key-guide"><dt><kbd>W</kbd> / <kbd>S</kbd></dt><dd>Increase / decrease throttle</dd><dt><kbd>A</kbd> / <kbd>D</kbd></dt><dd>Pitch trim; manual pitch when guidance is off</dd><dt><kbd>Space</kbd></dt><dd>Separate the active stage</dd><dt><kbd>G</kbd></dt><dd>Toggle ascent guidance</dd></dl><p>The first stage burns out; separate it to ignite the upper stage. After orbital insertion, release the upper stage to expose the spacecraft.</p></section><section><h3>03 / Dock manually</h3><p>After orbit, a separate terminal-approach scenario starts 70 m from a station in a circular reference orbit. It does not simulate the full rendezvous. Nose toward the station is −Z; RCS translation follows the spacecraft's body axes.</p><dl class="key-guide"><dt><kbd>W</kbd> / <kbd>S</kbd></dt><dd>Approach / retreat</dd><dt><kbd>A</kbd> / <kbd>D</kbd></dt><dd>Translate left / right</dd><dt><kbd>R</kbd> / <kbd>F</kbd></dt><dd>Translate up / down</dd><dt><kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd> <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd></dt><dd>Pitch / yaw</dd><dt><kbd>Q</kbd> / <kbd>E</kbd></dt><dd>Roll</dd><dt><kbd>Shift</kbd></dt><dd>Fine RCS control (20% thrust)</dd><dt><kbd>Space</kbd></dt><dd>Hold translation brake; consumes propellant</dd><dt><kbd>B</kbd></dt><dd>Toggle powered attitude hold</dd></dl><p>There is no passive vacuum braking. Counter-thrust cancels drift. Slow below 0.15 m/s closing speed before contact, align within 0.35 m and 5°, and keep rotation below 1°/s. All assists use propellant.</p></section><section><h3>04 / Land on another world</h3><p>Select a moon in assembly, inspect its globe, then start surface landing practice. This is a separate terminal descent with a standard fictional lander; launch stages and payload do not change this training vehicle. Transfers, ascent from the moon and sample collection are planned.</p><dl class="key-guide"><dt><kbd>W</kbd> / <kbd>S</kbd></dt><dd>Increase / decrease engine throttle</dd><dt><kbd>A</kbd> / <kbd>D</kbd></dt><dd>Tilt left / right (also left/right arrows)</dd><dt><kbd>B</kbd></dt><dd>Toggle powered upright attitude hold</dd><dt><kbd>Space</kbd></dt><dd>Cut engine throttle</dd></dl><p>Land within 25 m of the pad center, below 2 m/s vertical and 1.5 m/s lateral speed, within 8° of upright and below 3°/s rotation. These are game safety limits. Use the descent camera to inspect the zone; the external camera shows the lander. Small-body exercises begin lower and slower with thrust limited for controllability. Titan uses a simple dense-atmosphere drag model. Radiation, heat, tides, irregular gravity and terrain slopes are not survival models yet.</p><p>The globes use credited NASA/JPL image maps. Local landing terrain, clouds and spacecraft are illustrative procedural reconstructions rather than surveyed surface geometry. Orbital context images are shown for orientation rather than ephemeris-accurate geometry.</p></section><section><h3>Checkpoints &amp; pause</h3><p>Checkpoints save after launch preparation, staging and the terminal-scenario handoff. You can also save manually. <kbd>Esc</kbd> pauses. Leaving the game releases controls and pauses flight. Browser storage may be unavailable in private browsing.</p></section><section id="model-section"><h3>Flight model &amp; credits</h3><p>Fixed-step Newtonian flight, inverse-square Earth gravity, a simplified layered atmosphere, aerodynamic drag, propellant mass flow and stage mass changes. Docking uses local Hill relative motion near a circular orbit and rigid-body RCS rotation. No structural flex, Earth oblateness, full weather, ground-track plane change, long-range rendezvous or certification-grade contact mechanics.</p><p>The pad and station are illustrative; the spacecraft is fictional. Design limits and docking tolerances are game-specific values. Orbit is confirmed from energy and apsides, not an altitude timer.</p><p>Original game idea: <strong>Kinetic Core team</strong>. Implementation, procedural visuals and verification were assisted by Codex and specialist agents. No NASA affiliation or endorsement is implied.</p><p>Earth image: NASA Earth Observatory / NASA Goddard Space Flight Center; Blue Marble: Next Generation, produced by Reto Stöckli.</p><ul class="source-links"><li><a href="https://github.com/nasa/NASA-3D-Resources" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA 3D Resources: moon and planet visualization maps</a></li><li><a href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/4720/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA SVS: LROC Moon color and LOLA relief maps</a></li><li><a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/phys_par/sep.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JPL: satellite radii and gravitational parameters</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/blue-marble-next-generation/base-map/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA Earth Observatory: Blue Marble base map</a></li><li><a href="https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/ideal-rocket-equation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA Glenn: ideal rocket equation</a></li><li><a href="https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/specific-impulse/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA Glenn: specific impulse</a></li><li><a href="https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/earth-atmosphere-equation-metric/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA Glenn: atmosphere approximation</a></li><li><a href="https://www.spaceappschallenge.org/2026/challenges/space-mission-design-game/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA Space Apps: Space Mission Design Game</a></li><li><a href="https://threejs.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Three.js — MIT license</a></li></ul><p class="small-copy">Full equations, tests and limitations are included in docs/PHYSICS.md in the repository.</p></section></div></dialog>
